Abstract
In the present study, the various zeolites including hydrophilic Y zeolite, hydrophobic ZSM-5 zeolite and metal organic frameworks (MOFs) including MIL-101 and ZIF-8 were incorporated into the PLGA/chitosan nanofibers for controlled release of Paclitaxel anticancer drug against prostate cancer in vitro and in vivo. The synthesized nanoparticles and nanofibers were characterized using FTIR, XRD, SEM, BET and water contact angle analysis. The drug loading efficiency of nanofibers containing zeolites and MOFs indicated that the MOFs were more useful compared with zeolites for higher loading of Paclitaxel molecules. The Paclitaxel release
behavior from nanofibers containing zeolites and MOFs were also examined. The MTT assay and DAPI staining analysis were used to determine the cytotoxicity and apoptosis effect of nanofibers containing Paclitaxel against LNCaP prostate cancer cell lines.  The tumor inhibition rate in vivo were carried out to obtain the optimum nanofibrous formulation with maximum cell death percentage and tumor inhibition rate. The obtained results revealed the better activity of MOFs compared nanozeolites for higher loading of Paclitaxel drug into the nanoparticles and a more sustained release of drug from nanofibers containing MOFs.
Key words: PLGA/chitosan nanofibers, Zeolite, Metal organic framework, Paclitaxel, Prostate cancer
## 1. Introduction
The electrospun fibers produced from natural polymers with high porosity and fine pores have been developed as a drug delivery system for controlled release of anticancer drugs [1-5]. The nanofibers prepared by electrospinning method. The higher surface area and fine pores of nanofibers prepared by the electrospinning process provide the high efficient delivery of drugs and could increase the local drug concentration especially for the treatment of various cancers compared with other drug delivery systems (DDSs) such as nanoparticles, liposomes, micelles and hydrogels [1]. Furthermore, a sustained and controlled release of encapsulated drugs from nanofibers was achieved over other DDSs [2].  Chitosan as a biocompatible polysaccharide has been used as a pH-sensitive polymer was used as a carrier for targeted delivery of cancer agents [6, 7]. The electrospun chitosan nanofibers due to its high stretch characteristics and production of thinner fibers could incorporate the high content of dug molecules. However, the formation of uniform fibers from pure chitosan due to its lower viscosity and solution surface tension was difficult [8]. Furthermore, the use of electrospun chitosan nanofibers as an anticancer carrier due to its high swelling and the burst release of anticancer agents is limited. To overcome these problems, the blends of chitosan and polymers with high mechanical strength have been developed [9, 10]. Among various polymers, polylactic-co-glycolic acid (PLGA) as a biodegradable polymer with hydrophilic/hydrophobic nature can prevent the burst release of anticancer drugs and improve the mechanical properties of chitosan nanofibers [10].
Furthermore, the incorporation of inorganic fillers can increase the mechanical strength of natural fibers. Moreover the embedding of mineral compounds such as calcium carbonate, silica, gold, silver, clays, zeolites and metal organic frameworks (MOFs) into the polymeric nanofibers could significantly increase its biocompatibility, drug delivery capability and mechanical strength [11-14]. Zeolites as family of aluminosilicates with structures of $\mathrm{SiO_{4}}$ and $\mathrm{{AlO}_{4}}$ tetrahedra have been studied for biomedical applications such as DDSs due to its high loading efficiency, good biocompatibility, chemical stability, molecular sieve, and having the selective pores and channels for encapsulation of small molecules [14-18].  Zeolites with respect to Si/Al ratio, pore size, shape and hydrophilicity/hydrophobicity have been classified [19]. The zeolites with lower Si/Al ratios (1 to 5) indicate the hydrophilicity of the zeolite network which result in the faster release of drug molecules from zeolites in the buffer solution. Whereas, the slower release of drug molecules from zeolites with higher $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}/\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ ratio (10 to 100) could be attributed to the hydrophobicity of the zeolite matrix and the enhancement of the Van der Waals interactions which could hinder the drug molecules release in the solution [20]. Among hydrophilic zeolites, Y zeolite with a pore diameter of ${\sim}7\mathrm{~A}^{\circ}$ and inner cavity of ${\sim}12\mathrm{~A}^{\circ}$ has been used as an anticancer drug carrier [21-24]. ZSM-5 zeolite with a chemical formula of $\mathrm{Na_{n}A l_{n}S i_{96}\mathrm{-nO_{192}.16H_{2}O}}$ and mesoporous MFI acidic structure indicated the high potential for controlled release of anticancer drugs such as Doxorubicin (DOX) and 5-FU [25-28]. The use of nanozeolites in anticancer DDSs has advantages compared with microzeolites such as loading of higher content of drug into the nanozeolites pores, the direct transferring of drug molecules into the tumor cells and simultaneous co-delivery of several drugs with different release rates and various anticancer properties into the cancer tissues [29, 30]. To increase the zeolites performance in DDSs, the zeolitic polymer composites have been recently developed [21, 27, 31]. Karimi et al. [21] synthesized the polyethylene glycol (PEG) /zeolite Y, and PEG/zeolite ZSM-5 as drug carriers. They found that the PEG/ZSM-5 carrier exhibited as an efficient carrier for the controlled release of curcumin. Whereas, the hydrophilic nature of zeolite Y led to release a more curcumin molecules from composite. Wen et al. [27] synthesized the ZSM-5–DOX– chitosan pH-sensitive core-shell composite carrier against osteosarcoma. Salleh et al. [31] designed the zeolite Y/gelatin composite for controlled release of zerumbone natural anticancer drug.  The composite nanofibers containing zeolites have been recently developed for encapsulation of anticancer drugs [32, 33]. In the previous study, the potential of NaX zeolite/PLA/chitosan nanofibers was investigated for controlled release of DOX against carcinoma by our group [32]. However, there is a little study about incorporation of anticancer drugs/zeolites into the nanofibers for fabrication of composite nanofibers as anticancer drug carriers. The aim of the present study was to investigate the release properties of nanofibrous containing Y and ZSM-5 zeolites nanoparticles. The loading of NaY and ZSM-5 zeolites into the nanofibers could control the release rate of Paclitaxel from nanofibers through the enhancement/decrease in the hydrophilicity/hydrophobicity of nanofibers [31, 32].
Among various nanoparticles used in DDSs, the nanometal organic frameworks (NMOFs) as a new class of crystalline porous materials due to having tunable pore sizes and large porosity, high specific surface area and good compatibility as well as lower toxicity toward normal cells via the selection of biocompatible metals and organic ligands, have been developed for controlled release and targeted delivery of anticancer drugs [33-45]. Among several types of NMOFs, the stable NMOFs in phosphate buffer saline (PBS, $\mathrm{pH}=7.4\$ ) solution such as ZIF, MIL and UiO based-NMOFs were utilized for biomedicine applications [46]. However, the use of pristine NMOFs due to difficult reversibility of NMOFs is limited. On the other hand, the use of pH-sensitive carrier cause to release the anticancer drugs on the acidic environment of tumor cells (5.4-6.8) [40]. Therefore, coating of NMOFs with pH-sensitive polymers could be resulted to release the anticancer drugs on the target cancerous tissues [47, 48]. Furthermore, the structure of NMOFs such as ZIF-8 and UiO-66 has been decomposed under acidic condition and the drugs were easily released from NMOFs in acidic environment [49-51]. Nejhadshafiee et al. [52] coated the surface of NMOFs with folic acid-chitosan conjugate to design the pH sensitive carrier for targeted delivery of curcumin (CUR) and 5-fluorouracil (5-FU) against breast cancer cells death. Recently, the MOF nanoparticles have been incorporated into the nanofibers [53-55]. For examples, the performance of UiO-66 MOF nanoparticles loaded-chitosan/poly ethylene oxide/polyurethane core-shell nanofibers was investigated for the sustained release of DOX and folic acid against breast cancer [53]. The aim of this study was to compare the potential of Paclitaxel loaded-Y zeolite, ZSM-5 zeolite, MIL-101 MOF and ZIF-8 incorporatedPLGA/chitosan nanofibers for targeted delivery of Paclitaxel molecules against prostate cancer. The prepared zeolite/ Paclitaxel and MOF/ Paclitaxel loaded- PLGA/chitosan/zeolite/ Paclitaxel were characterized using FTIR, XRD, SEM, BET and water contact angle analysis. The drug loading efficiency of nanofibers containing zeolites and MOF nanoparticles were evaluated to obtain the optimum carrier for maximum loading of anticancer drug. The Paclitaxel release profiles of nanofibers containing zeolites and MOFs were investigated to determine the effect of zeolite and MOF content on the release behavior of Paclitaxel. The in vitro cytotoxicity of samples and the tumor inhibition rate in vivo were carried out to obtain the optimum formulation with maximum cell death percentage and tumor inhibition rate.
## 2. Experimental
## 2.1 Materials
Poly(D,L-lactide-co-glycolide) (PLGA; lactide:glycolide (75:25), $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{w}_{\mathrm{av}}$ : 66-107 KDa), and chitosan ( $96\%$ de-acetylated; $\mathbf{M}\mathrm{w}_{\mathrm{av}}$ : $200\mathrm{kDa}$ ) purchased from Sigma-Aldrich (USA) were utilized to synthesize the PLGA/chitosan nanofibers. $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}.9\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ purchased from Sigma-Aldrich (USA) were used for synthesis of MIL-101 MOF. Sodium hydroxide (NaOH) and sodium aluminate $(\mathrm{NaAl}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3})$ ) supplied from Sigma-Aldrich were used to prepare NaY nanozeolites. To synthesize the ZIF-8 NMOFs, zinc(II) nitrate hexahydrate $(\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ and 2-methylimidazole provided from Sigma-Aldrich were used. Tetrapropylammonium hydroxide solution (TPAOH), tetra-ethyl orthosilicate (TEOS), and aluminum nitrate $(\mathrm{Al}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3},$ ) supplied from Sigma-Aldrich were applied for synthesis of ZSMnanozeolites.
## 2.2 Synthesis of nanozeolites
NaY zeolite was synthesized via microwave heating method from molar composition of 10 $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ :1 ${\bf A l}_{2}{\bf O}_{3}$ :14 Na2O:800 $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ as described previously [56]. Briefly, the predetermined amounts of NaOH and ${\mathrm{NaAl}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{3}$ were mixed in deionized water at $70^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{\textrm{1h}}$ . Then, the product was cooled at room temperature and following the sodium silicate solution was added into the mixture under stirring for $24\mathrm{h}$ . Then, the mixture was poured into a polypropylene vessel and was dried in an oven at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $48\mathrm{{h}}$ . The ZSM-5 nanozeolites were synthesized from molar composition of $0.5\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ , 120.5 TEOS, 0.6024 $\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , 30.12 TPAH and $2313.25\:\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ by hydrothermal method as described previously [57].
## 2.3 Synthesis of NMOFs
To synthesize MIL-101 MOF, the predetermined amounts of $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}.9\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and H2BDC were mixed in water for $30\mathrm{min}$ . Then, the obtained mixture was poured into a stainless-steel autoclave and following was placed in an oven at $220^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $18\mathrm{h}$ . Then, the obtained product was centrifuged and washed several times with deionized water followed by washing with methanol and acetone to remove H2BDC. The DMF was added to the resulting powder and kept at $70^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for overnight. Finally, the product was dried in a vacuum oven at $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ [58]. $\mathrm{Zn(NO_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}$ and 2-methylimidazole were mixed in methanol under stirring for $6\mathrm{{h}}$ followed by drying in an oven at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ for  synthesis of  ZIF-8 MOF nanoparticles [59].
## 2.4 Synthesis of PLGA/chitosan and NMOFs/nanozeolites loaded-nanofibers
To synthesize the PLGA/chitosan nanofibers, 4 wt. $\%$ chitosan dissolved in acetic acid under stirring for $6\mathrm{{h}}$ and was transferred in one syringe. Then, $16\mathrm{\mt.}\%$ PLGA dissolved in chloroform/DMF $(9{:}1\ \mathrm{v}/\mathrm{v})$ under stirring for $6\mathrm{{h}}$ was transferred into the another syringe and the two syringes were cited at either side of the collector with tip-collector distance of $15\mathrm{cm}$ , high voltages of $16~\mathrm{kV}$ and flow rate of $0.25\mathrm{mLh}^{-1}$ for production of PLGA/chitosan nanofibers [60]. To synthesize the PLGA/chitosan/MOF and PLGA/chitosan/zeolite nanofibers, $20\%$ concentrations of ZIF-8, MIL-101 MOF and NaY, ZSM-5 nanozeolites (by weight of polymer) were dispersed into the chitosan solution and were degassed in an ultrasonic bath for $10\mathrm{min}$ before electrospinning process. To mix the Paclitaxel molecules and synthesized MOFs and
zeolites, $0.1\ \mathrm{g}$ of MOFs/zeolites were added in $10~\mathrm{mL}$ DMF containing $20\mathrm{mg}$ Paclitaxel under stirring for $24\mathrm{h}$ .  Then, the samples were centrifuged for $20\mathrm{min}$ $(5000\mathrm{rpm})$ and finally were washed with distilled water and ethanol.  The final content of Paclitaxel in the nanozeolites and NMOFs was determined after centrifugation of samples using HPLC at wavelength of $230\mathrm{nm}$ , respectively. To dope the Paclitaxel, nanozeolites and NMOFs into the nanofibers, the synthesized zeolite, and NMOFs were dispersed into the chitosan solution containing $20\mathrm{mg}$ Paclitaxel before electrospinning process. The schematic of the synthesis of NMOFs/nanozeolites-Paclitaxel loaded-nanofibers is illustrated in Fig.1.
