## 2.5 Characterization tests
The Fourier transform infrared analyses (FTIR) on the synthesized nanofibers was carried out using an Equinox 55 FTIR spectrometer ranging from $4000{\cdot}400\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ .  The $\mathrm{\Delta}X$ -ray powder diffraction (XRD) analysis on the synthesized nanozeolites, NMOFs, and nanofibers was conducted using Philips X’pert diffractometer in the range of $10–80^{\circ}$ . The scanning electron microscopy (SEM) on the synthesized nanozeolites, NMOFs, and nanofibers was conducted using SEM, JEOL JSM-6380 instrument after gold coating. The size distribution of particles and fibers were calculated using Image J software (Image-Proplus, Media Cybrernetics).
## 2.6 Drug loading efficiency, drug release and kinetic studies
The drug loading efficiency (DLE $\%$ ) was evaluated by measuring the final content of drugs in the composite nanofibers in comparison to initial content of drugs in nanofibers as follows:
The PTX release profiles of nanofibers containing various contents of zeolites and MOFs was achieved by immersion of $10\mathrm{mg}$ of nanofibrous samples in $20~\mathrm{mL}$ of PBS solution under shaking speed of $100\mathrm{rpm}$ , temperature of $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and various pH values of 5.5 and 7.4. At certain times, $1\mathrm{mL}$ of incubation solution was transferred for HPLC (Agilent 1260 HPLC System) absorbance measurement and replaced with $1\mathrm{mL}$ of PBS [61, 62].  All experiments were conducted three times and the average values were reported.
## 2.7 Cell viability
The cytotoxicity of synthesized Paclitaxel/zeolites and Paclitaxel/NMOFs loaded-nanofibers in vitro condition was investigated via MTT assay by incubation of Paclitaxel and Paclitaxel loaded nanofibers in LNCaP cells $(100~\mathrm{\textmug.mL^{-1}},$ ) provided from Pasteur Institute of Iran under $5\%\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{72\mathrm{{h}}}$ as described previously [63, 64]. Annexin VFITC/PI double staining flow cytometry analysis was used to analyze the morphological changes of LNCaP cells in the presence of synthesized nanofibrous samples after $120\mathrm{h}$ incubation time using a CyFlow flowcytometer (Partec, Germany) as described previously [53].
To investigate the performance of samples in vivo, $2{\times}10^{6}$ LNCaP cells were injected into the right flanks of each mouse to reach the tumor volume in the range $150{-}200~\mathrm{mm}^{3}$ . Then, the tumor-bearing mice (40 mice) were divided into 8 groups  which well treated with Free Paclitaxel, Pure nanofibers, Paclitaxel loaded-nanofibers, Paclitaxel/ZSM-5 loaded-nanofibers, Paclitaxel/NaY loaded-nanofibers, Paclitaxel/ MIL-101 loaded-nanofibers, Paclitaxel/ ZIF-8 loaded-nanofibers and pure PBS as control. The Paclitaxel dose was $20\mathrm{mg/kg}$ . Briefly, animals were anesthetized by intra-peritoneal injection of pentobarbital at $20\mathrm{mg/kg}$ , and a small incision was made on the skin to expose the tumor. A small incision was made into the tumor, and synthesized fibers were inserted into the tumor and then the wound was closed using
subcutaneous suturing. The tumor volume and the tumor inhibition rate (TIR) were calculated as follows:
Tumor volume $\c=$ (The longest diameter $\times$ the shortest diameter) $^2/2$ (Eq.3) TI $\mathrm{\sfR}(\%)=$ Tumor weight of control group - Tumor weight of sample group)/Tumor weight of control group $\times100$ (Eq.4)
The statistical analyses were carried out using SPSS16 and the statistical significant was carried out at $\mathrm{p}{<}0.05$ . Student’s t-test was used to analyze the significant difference between the responses. A p-value $<0.05$ was considered statistically significant.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1 Characterization of nanozeolites, NMOFs and nanofibers
The XRD patterns of synthesized ZSM-5, NaY nanozeolites and MIL-101, ZIF-8 NMOFs are illustrated in Fig.2. The diffraction peaks of synthesized NMOFs and nanozeolites exhibited the successful synthesis of MIL-101, ZIF-8 NMOFs and ZSM-5, NaY nanozeolites [56-59]. The SEM images of ZSM-5, NaY nanozeolites and MIL-101, ZIF-8 NMOFs are illustrated in Fig.3. The cubic structure of crystals with an average particle size of $55\mathrm{nm}$ , was obtained for MIL-101 MOF (Fig.3a). The uniform rhombic dodecahedrons structure of ZIF-8 crystals with an average particle size of $45\mathrm{nm}$ was achieved (Fig.3b). The cubic-like and spherical shapes of NaY nanozeolites in the range $50\mathrm{-}150~\mathrm{nm}$ with an average particle size of about $95\mathrm{nm}$ were synthesized (Fig.3c). The spherical structure of ZSM-5 crystals were observed with a size distribution ranging from 30 to $100\mathrm{nm}$ and an average particle size of $75\mathrm{nm}$ (Fig.3d). The surface morphology of PLGA/chitosan nanofibers, PLGA/chitosan/Paclitaxel PLGA/chitosan/ZIF-8-/Paclitaxel, PLGA/chitosan/MIL-101-/Paclitaxel, PLGA/chitosan/NaY/Paclitaxel and PLGA/chitosan/ZSM-5-/Paclitaxel composite nanofibers are illustrated in Fig.4.
As shown, the smooth fibers without any beads with average diameter of $\sim230\mathrm{nm}$ was found for PLGA/chitosan nanofiber. Whereas, the average diameter was increased to $\sim290\mathrm{nm}$ by loading of Paclitaxel molecules into the PLGA/chitosan fibers. The similar morphologies with inter connected pores were obtained for both PLGA/chitosan and PLGA/chitosan/Paclitaxel nanofibers which demonstrated the uniform distribution of Paclitaxel molecules into the PLGA/chitosan solution. Thus, the physical loading of Paclitaxel molecules into the PLGA/chitosan nanofibers resulted in gradual increasing of fiber diameter. The incorporation of ZIF-8-/Paclitaxel, MIL-101-/Paclitaxel, ZSM-5-/Paclitaxel and NaY-/Paclitaxel into the PLGA/chitosan solution led to fabrication of nanofibers with average diameters of 340, 370, 395 and $410\mathrm{nm}$ , respectively. The fiber morphology was gradually changed from smooth to beaded fibers by doping of drug loaded-nanozeolites and NMOFs into the PLGA/chitosan nanofibers. The increase in the viscosity of solution and formation of the higher charge density on the polymer jet resulted in increasing of fiber diameter and formation of beaded fibers. The presence of some nanozeolites and NMOFs on the nanofibers surface could be attributed to aggregation of some nanomaterials in the PLGA/chitosan solution before electrospinning process. The crosssection image of PLGA/chitosan nanofibers indicated that the thickness of nanofibers was found to be $15{\pm}3~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ (Fig. 4g).
The BET surface area of synthesized NaY nanozeolite, ZSM-5 nanozeolite, ZIF-8 NMOFs and MIL-101 NMOFs were found to be 413.2, 335.1, 812.3 and $1875\mathrm{m}^{2}.\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively. The obtained results revealed the higher surface area of NMOFs compared with nanozeolites. The water contact angle of synthesized PLGA/chitosan, MIL-101/Paclitaxel, ZIF-8/Paclitaxel, NaY/Paclitaxel and ZSM-5/Paclitaxel loaded- PLGA/chitosan nanofibers were found to be $45{\pm}3.5^{\circ}$ , $26{\pm}4.2^{\circ}$ , $65{\pm}5.3^{\circ}$ , $31{\pm}5.5^{\circ}$ and $86{\pm}4.5^{\circ}$ , respectively. (Fig. 4h). The obtained results indicated an enhancement in the hydrophilicity of PLGA/chitosan nanofibers by incorporation of NaY nanozeolite and MIL-101 due to increasing the hydroxyl groups on the nanofiber surface. Whereas, the increase in the hydrophobicity of PLGA/chitosan nanofibers was observed by loading of ZSM-5 nanozeolite, and ZIF-8 NMOF due to hydrophobic nature of ZSM-5 nanozeolite and ZIF-8 NMOFs.
