The drug release profiles of PLGA/chitosan/Paclitaxel, PLGA/chitosan/ZIF-8/Paclitaxel, PLGA/chitosan/MIL-101/Paclitaxel, PLGA/chitosan/NaY/Paclitaxel and PLGA/chitosan/ZSM5/Paclitaxel nanofibers under acidic pH (5.5) and physiological pH (7.4) are illustrated in Fig.6.
As shown, the drug release rate from all synthesized carriers under acidic pH was higher than that of pH of 7.4. The protonation of amino and hydroxyl groups of chitosan and its more swelling under acidic pH as well as the relative decomposition of synthesized zeolites and NMOFs at pH of 5.5 resulted in faster release of Paclitaxel molecules from nanofibers under acidic pH [8, 46]. For PLGA/chitosan nanofibers, the initial burst release was due to Paclitaxel molecules release from nanofibers surface. By loading of Paclitaxel into the MIL-101 MOF and NaY zeolite, the initial burst release was removed and the continuous release from nanofibers was achieved. The Paclitaxel release from nanofibers containing MOFs and zeolites have been occurred in two stages including the paclitaxel molecules release from MOFs and zeolites followed by release of paclitaxel from pores of nanofibers which resulted in the continuous release of Paclitaxel without burst release. However, the release rate due to increasing the hydrophilicity of nanofibers was increased and $80\%$ of Paclitaxel drug molecules were released from PLGA/chitosan, PLGA/chitosan/NaY and PLGA/chitosan/MIL-101 nanofibers during 144 h, 120 h and $80\mathrm{h}$ , respectively under acidic pH and 240 h, 168 h, $120\mathrm{h}$ , respectively under physiological pH. By loading of ZSM-5 and ZIF-8 hydrophobic nanoparticles into the PLGA/chitosan nanofibers, the slower release of Paclitaxel was obtained during $360\mathrm{h}$ , and $480\mathrm{h}$ using PLGA/chitosan/ZIF-8 and PLGA/chitosan/ ZSM-5 nanofibers under acidic pH and $600\mathrm{h}$ , and $720\mathrm{h}$ , respectively under physiological pH. The faster release of Paclitacel molecules from PLGA/chitosan/MIL-101 nanofibers compared with PLGA/chitosan/NaY nanofibers and from PLGA/chitosan/ ZIF-8 compared with PLGA/chitosan/ ZSM-5 nanofibers could be attributed to the higher drug loading efficiency of NMOFs loaded-PLGA/chitosan nanofibers and the lower distance of drug molecules in the NMOFs matrix. Therefore, the release rate of synthesized nanocarriers for Paclitaxel release was in order of PLGA/chitosan/MIL-101(pH: 5.5)>
PLGA/chitosan/NaY (pH: 5.5)- PLGA/chitosan/MIL-101 (pH: 7.4)> PLGA/chitosan(pH: 5.5)> PLGA/chitosan/NaY (pH: 7.4)> PLGA/chitosan(pH: 7.4) $>$ PLGA/chitosan/ZIF-8 (pH: 5.5)> PLGA/chitosan/ZSM-5 (pH: 5.5)> PLGA/chitosan/ZIF-8 (pH: 7.4)> PLGA/chitosan/ZSM-5 (pH: 7.4).
